AG2-PACT: 

A Falsification-First Framework for Evaluating Cell-State-Aware Evidence Provenance and Distance-Dependent Reliability in Conditional Genomic Foundation Models

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

Conditional genomic foundation models such as AlphaGenome 2 (AG2) produce variant-effect and regulatory predictions at scale, yet the reliability of those predictions is neither uniform across genomic distance nor transparent with respect to the provenance of the underlying measurements. This dissertation develops and formally specifies AG2-PACT, a falsification-first evaluation framework designed to test whether cell-state-aware evidence provenance and distance-dependent reliability assessment improve the experimental validation efficiency of conditional genomic foundation-model predictions beyond raw sequence-to-function scores. The framework separates two categorically distinct inference tracks, isolates calibration from rank and magnitude as three non-interchangeable measurement constructs, models provenance as a three-level variable grounded in the API's documented imputation semantics, and adopts a three-part decision rule in which equivalence and inconclusive outcomes are first-class results. Statistical inference is conducted with wild cluster bootstrap procedures appropriate to small and imbalanced cluster structures, with equivalence margins derived from an explicit cost model rather than asserted. Reproducibility is protected by a fixed canary set and full request logging against a hosted, non-version-pinned model. The dissertation specifies a composite falsification condition under which the framework's own value is refuted. The central commitment is that the programme must be able to demonstrate its own irrelevance; a framework that can only confirm its utility is a persuasion device, not a scientific instrument.

---

1. Introduction and Problem Statement

1.1 The evaluation gap

Genomic foundation models are increasingly deployed as oracles for variant interpretation, enhancer–gene linking, and regulatory design. Their outputs are consumed by experimentalists who must decide which hypotheses to test. The practical question is therefore not whether a model correlates with held-out measurements, but whether model-derived evidence improves the experimental validation efficiency of a downstream prioritisation decision.

This distinction is not cosmetic. A model may achieve high rank correlation while systematically compressing effect magnitudes. It may predict direction correctly on average while reversing direction in distal regulatory regions. It may achieve strong discrimination on a binary task while failing to calibrate probability. Each of these failures has different consequences for a validation pipeline, and each requires a different corrective instrument.

1.2 The two deficiencies

AG2-PACT is motivated by two hypothesised deficiencies in current conditional genomic foundation models.

Deficiency 1 — Distance-dependent magnitude miscalibration. The model may systematically underpredict the magnitude of effects mediated by distal regulatory elements, with the shortfall growing as a function of enhancer–transcription-start-site distance. This is a calibration deficiency, not a rank deficiency: the model may rank distal elements correctly while compressing their magnitudes.

Deficiency 2 — Provenance-insensitive reliability. The model may assign comparable confidence to predictions grounded in directly observed measurements and to predictions grounded in imputed or exploratory channels. If so, a downstream consumer cannot distinguish evidence from hypothesis.

1.3 The central falsifiable question

The dissertation addresses one question, stated once for the record:

Can cell-state-aware evidence provenance and distance-dependent reliability assessment improve the experimental validation efficiency of conditional genomic foundation-model predictions beyond raw sequence-to-function scores?

If the answer is affirmative, the contribution is the evidence layer. If the answer is negative, the contribution is the demonstration that the layer is unnecessary. Both outcomes are scientific. The framework is designed so that neither outcome can be distorted into the other.

1.4 Scope and epistemic stance

The programme is falsification-first. It commits in advance to reporting null, equivalent, and inconclusive results. It commits to a three-part decision structure in which the absence of statistical significance is never reported as evidence of absence. It commits to a composite falsification condition under which the framework's own contribution is refuted. These commitments are not rhetorical; they are encoded in the decision rules of Sections 4 and 8.

