2. Theoretical Framework

2.1 Three-layer architecture

The framework is organised into three layers.

Layer 1 — Predictive layer. The base model, denoted f_\theta, maps a genomic input x and a conditioning state c to a prediction \hat{y} = f_\theta(x, c).

Layer 2 — Contextual layer. A context function g maps the input, the conditioning state, and the provenance metadata of the underlying tracks to a reliability estimate r = g(x, c, \pi), where \pi denotes the provenance configuration.

Layer 3 — Utility layer. A decision function h maps the predictive output and the reliability estimate to a prioritisation score u = h(\hat{y}, r), which is evaluated against an experimental validation criterion.

The dissertation tests whether Layer 2 and Layer 3 add value beyond Layer 1 alone. This is the framework's own falsification target.

2.2 Track separation

Two categorically distinct inference tracks are maintained and never pooled.

Track A — Variant-effect inference. The input is a DNA variant; the output is a predicted molecular consequence. Track A datasets are evaluated for in-distribution calibration only; datasets that appear in the model's training corpus are excluded from confirmatory inference.

Track B — Perturbation inference. The input is a controlled experimental intervention (CRISPRi, CRISPRa, TAP-seq); the output is a measured transcriptional consequence. Track B datasets are evaluated for prioritisation utility.

The separation is non-negotiable. A CRISPRi ablation and a DNA variant are different biological estimands. Treating the difference between them as a calibration error is a category error dressed as a metric.

2.3 The finite transformation set

Because Track B perturbations and Track A variant scores are not the same quantity, any comparison between them requires an explicit transformation. The transformation set \mathcal{T} is finite and pre-specified:

\mathcal{T} = \{\,\text{identity},\ \text{rank},\ \text{linear},\ \text{sigmoid},\ \text{none}\,\}.

No transformation outside \mathcal{T} may be applied after evaluation data are observed. A transformation chosen post hoc is not a transformation; it is a fit to the evaluation set.

