4. Hypotheses and Decision Rules

4.1 H1 — Distance-dependent magnitude miscalibration

Statement. Conditional genomic foundation models exhibit distance-dependent magnitude miscalibration, and the miscalibration differs between model generations.

Status. The distal underprediction phenomenon is documented in the independent literature for this model family. H1 is therefore partly a replication hypothesis and partly a quantification-and-correction hypothesis. The confirmatory contribution is the estimation of \Delta\gamma_3 with a clustered bootstrap interval, not the discovery of distal underprediction.

Decision rule. A three-part structure:

\text{H1} \in \{\,\text{supported},\ \text{equivalent},\ \text{inconclusive}\,\}.

Supported if the confidence interval for \Delta\gamma_3 excludes zero and the estimated effect exceeds the minimum detectable effect. Equivalent if the two-one-sided-tests procedure rejects both one-sided nulls at the pre-specified margin \delta^{\mathrm{eq}}. Inconclusive otherwise. A non-significant result is never reported as falsification.

4.2 H2 — Cell-state conditioning

H2 is split into two claims with different evidential status.

H2a — Within-K562 conditioning. Matched K562 conditioning improves Track-B prediction relative to a matched control. This is testable with available data.

H2b — Cross-state generalisation. The gain generalises to a non-K562 cell state. This is untested until a high-quality non-K562 perturbation resource is secured. H2b is marked as untested in every abstract, figure, and preregistration statement. The search for such a resource is a gating requirement for any generalisation claim.

Direction and magnitude are reported separately; H2a is supported only if both exceed the minimum detectable effect.

4.3 H3 — Modality provenance

Observed-versus-imputed status predicts reliability, estimated separately across the three imputation modes with the monotonicity expectation of Section 3.5. The operational primitive is the observed-track filter; the audit verifies whether it returns a strict subset, a null filter, or a collapse, and whether per-track metadata remain consistent after filtering.

4.4 H4 — Evidence-based prioritisation

The evidence layer improves validation yield and biological utility over raw scores. The decision is two-part:

\text{H4} \in \{\,\text{superior},\ \text{equivalent},\ \text{inconclusive}\,\}.

Superior if \Delta@k exceeds zero beyond the minimum detectable effect. Equivalent if the two-one-sided-tests procedure rejects both one-sided nulls at the utility equivalence margin. A small-but-real effect is therefore not misreported as null.

4.5 Component ablation

A ten-row ablation table reports each component's contribution under both additive and leave-one-out designs, together with a direct provenance-ablation test. The purpose is to permit the framework to deny credit to provenance if provenance does not earn it.

