6. Statistical Analysis Plan

6.1 Small-cluster inference

Cluster-robust standard errors are unreliable when the number of clusters is small and the cluster sizes are imbalanced. The primary inference procedure is the wild cluster bootstrap with six-point weights, appropriate when the number of clusters is below approximately twelve. For intermediate cluster counts, the six-point procedure remains the primary method, and a score-transformed variant is reported as a sensitivity check. The analysis reports the raw cluster count, the cluster-size distribution, and the effective number of clusters.

6.2 Power

Minimum detectable effects are computed at a two-sided \alpha = 0.05 and power 0.80, under the observed cluster-size distribution of the chosen Track-B datasets rather than a balanced assumption. A balanced-cluster power calculation is optimistic for the imbalanced structure that the datasets actually exhibit.

6.3 Equivalence margins

Equivalence margins are derived, not asserted. The directional margin for H1 follows from the smallest distance-interaction difference of scientific interest, anchored to the independent benchmark literature. The utility margin for H4 follows from a cost model: the validation-yield gain that justifies the evidence layer's compute, API, and analyst cost. Until the derivation is recorded, both margins are provisional and are not treated as frozen constants.

Equivalence is concluded only through a two-one-sided-tests procedure in which both one-sided nulls are rejected at the pre-specified margin.

6.4 Multiplicity

Secondary subgroup analyses are controlled by the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure at a pre-specified false-discovery rate. Primary hypotheses are not adjusted; each carries its own decision rule.

6.5 Missing data

Complete-case logging is used. Experimental outcomes are never imputed.

---

7. Reproducibility and Provenance Controls

7.1 Hosted-model reproducibility

The model is served through a hosted API whose weights are not presently released. The hosted model is not version-pinned. Bit-identical outputs on a single day do not constitute a version guarantee.

Every API call logs the client version, the returned model-version string where available, the condition identifier, the request timestamp, and the full request payload. Raw outputs are stored, not only derived scores, so that a later model change can be detected by re-querying a fixed canary set.

7.2 The canary set

A fixed canary set of fifty to one hundred pairs is queried at the start and at the end of the evaluation period. If the canary outputs drift, the evaluation is paused and the drift is reported. Without this control, a null utility result could reflect a silent model update rather than a property of the evidence layer.

7.3 Leakage controls

The four leakage controls of Section 3.7 are frozen in the configuration file before any evaluation-set inference. Leakage is silent; a seed, a modality map, a value of k, or a normalisation constant chosen after seeing evaluation data renders the metric partially in-sample, and no post-hoc correction recovers it.

7.4 Perturbation-operator discipline

The in-silico perturbation operator is pre-specified on distributional grounds: the operator must preserve the composition of the input sufficiently that the perturbed sequence is not out-of-distribution. The composition-preserving shuffle is the primary operator. At least one alternative operator is reported as a sensitivity analysis. If the primary results do not survive the operator sensitivity analysis, that fragility is reported.

7.5 Fixed pair set

Comparisons between model generations are conducted on a fixed scored pair set. A comparison that allows the larger-context model to score a different pair set is confounded by context length, and the apparent advantage of the larger model may be an artefact of seeing more sequence.

---

8. Falsification Conditions

8.1 Composite falsification

The framework's value is falsified if, on the primary dataset with the pair set held fixed, all three of the following hold:

1. the evidence layer does not improve validation yield beyond the utility equivalence margin;
2. the provenance contrast is flat across the three imputation modes; and
3. the distance interaction is explained by a single global calibration slope rather than a distance-dependent term.

Together, these three nulls would show that the layer adds complexity without predictive gain. The composite condition is stated in the preregistration so that a null result is interpretable as a finding rather than as an ambiguous failure.

8.2 Component-level falsification

Independently of the composite condition, provenance is denied credit if its component does not survive the leave-one-out ablation. The framework is permitted — indeed required — to report that its own evidence layer is unnecessary.

8.3 Reporting discipline

Negative, equivalent, and inconclusive results are all reportable. The three-part decision structure exists precisely so that no result is forced into a false binary. The written narrative leads with the Track-B perturbation outcomes; developer-benchmark diagnostics are reported as secondary calibration checks and remain visibly separated from confirmatory claims.

---

9. Expected Contributions and Limitations

9.1 Contributions

The dissertation contributes (i) a formal separation of calibration, rank, and magnitude as distinct measurement constructs with distinct decision-theoretic consequences; (ii) a provenance model grounded in the three-level imputation semantics of the target API rather than in a binary observed/imputed fiction; (iii) a small-cluster inference protocol appropriate to the imbalanced structure of enhancer–perturbation data; (iv) a derivation-based rather than assertion-based treatment of equivalence margins; and (v) a composite falsification condition that renders the framework's own irrelevance a reportable scientific outcome.

9.2 Limitations

Three limitations are acknowledged in advance and are not discovered post hoc.

Cell-line concentration. The Track-B evidence base is concentrated in a single leukaemia line. H2b is untested. Any claim of cross-state generalisation awaits a non-K562 perturbation resource of usable scale.

Hosted-model opacity. The model is not version-pinned and its weights are unreleased. The canary set and request logging mitigate but do not eliminate the risk that a silent update contaminates the evaluation.

Estimand heterogeneity. The Track-B resources measure different perturbation modalities with different readouts. Harmonisation to a common estimand is a modelling decision with irreducible residual heterogeneity, which is disclosed rather than assumed away.

9.3 Prospective extension

A minimal prospective element — held-out loci or a small new perturbation pilot — is designed after the retrospective analysis so that the utility endpoint is not purely post hoc. The prospective element is reported separately and is not pooled with retrospective results.

---

10. Conclusion

AG2-PACT is a falsification-first evaluation framework whose central commitment is that it must be able to demonstrate its own irrelevance. Every structural decision follows from that commitment. The three-part decision rule exists so that no result is forced into a false binary. The equivalence clauses exist so that a small-but-real effect is not misreported as null. The component ablation exists so that provenance can be denied credit if it does not earn it. The preregistration gate exists so that the evidence score cannot be tuned to the evaluation set. The observed-track fallback exists so that a missing API primitive cannot be replaced by a plausible-sounding proxy. The track separation exists so that a category error cannot be dressed as a calibration metric. The contamination audit exists so that a zero-shot claim is not contaminated by training data. The canary set exists so that a silent model update cannot masquerade as a scientific finding.

The programme does not set out to show that its evidence layer improves the base model. It sets out to test whether it does, and to report the outcome either way without distortion. If the layer improves validation efficiency beyond a derived margin, the contribution is the layer. If it does not, the contribution is the demonstration that the layer is unnecessary. Both are scientific. The framework is built so that neither can be mistaken for the other.

