Appendix B — Formal Definitions

B.1 Direction accuracy

\mathrm{DA} = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\!\left[\operatorname{sign}(\hat{y}_i)=\operatorname{sign}(y_i^{\exp})\right].

\mathrm{DA} measures sign agreement only. Where a binary task exists, \mathrm{auROC} is reported separately and is never substituted for \mathrm{DA}. The two quantities answer different questions and are never treated as interchangeable.

B.2 Rank correlation

Spearman's \rho_s measures agreement of order only. It carries no information about magnitude. A model with \rho_s=0.95 may compress all effects by a constant factor and remain rank-perfect.

B.3 Magnitude error

\mathrm{MAE}=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left|\hat{y}_i-y_i^{\exp}\right|,\qquad
\mathrm{RMSE}=\sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\left(\hat{y}_i-y_i^{\exp}\right)^2}.

B.4 Calibration model

y^{\exp}=\alpha+\beta\hat{y}+\varepsilon .

Direction convention, fixed throughout: \beta>1 ⇒ underestimation of magnitude; \beta<1 ⇒ overestimation. This convention is not reversed in any derived quantity.

B.5 Crossing point

\hat{y}^{*}=-\frac{\alpha}{\beta-1}.

Delta-method variance:

\operatorname{Var}(\hat{y}^{*})\approx
\frac{1}{(\beta-1)^2}\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\alpha})
+\frac{\alpha^2}{(\beta-1)^4}\operatorname{Var}(\hat{\beta})
-\frac{2\alpha}{(\beta-1)^3}\operatorname{Cov}(\hat{\alpha},\hat{\beta}).

Reported with a bootstrap interval. Identification rule: if the interval for \hat{y}^{*} covers the entire observed predictor range, the result is reported as no crossing identified. A point estimate is never reported as a discovery in that case.

Region-wise reporting. Fraction of observations above and below \hat{y}^{*}, together with region-wise underprediction fractions, are always reported. No dataset is classified from \beta alone.

B.6 Distance-dependent miscalibration

y_i^{\exp}=\alpha+\beta\hat{y}_i+\gamma_1 d_i+\gamma_2(\hat{y}_i d_i)+\gamma_3 d_i^{2}+\varepsilon_i,
\qquad
\Delta\gamma_3=\gamma_3^{\mathrm{AG2}}-\gamma_3^{\mathrm{AG1}}.

\Delta\gamma_3 is estimated directly with a clustered bootstrap interval. Two separately estimated coefficients with differing significance are not evidence of a difference between them.

B.7 Controlled modality ablation effect

\mathrm{CMAE}=\mathbb{E}\!\left[S_{\mathrm{obs}}-S_{\mathrm{mask}}\right].

Validity gates, both required before any CMAE is reported:

· C1 (no OOD collapse): the masked-condition output distribution must not shift beyond a pre-specified quantitative threshold \tau_{C1}, fixed before analysis.
· C2 (held constant): all non-masked inputs identical across conditions.

Mandatory controls:

1. Null ablation — ablate a modality irrelevant to the prediction; report its CMAE alongside the substantive one.
2. Imputation redundancy — compare masking with imputation. A small CMAE under redundancy indicates internal reconstruction, not unimportance of the modality.
3. Shuffled-modality ablation — ablation of a permuted version of the modality; if it also moves the prediction, the operator rather than the information is driving the effect.

B.8 Imputation-reliability gap (observational)

\mathrm{IRG}=\mathbb{E}[S\mid I=1]-\mathbb{E}[S\mid I=0].

Explicitly observational. Never reported with causal language. Scope of causal language is restricted to the model-input intervention actually performed, which is CMAE.

B.9 Provenance contrasts (three-level)

\pi\in\{\texttt{observed},\ \texttt{imputed\_supported},\ \texttt{imputed\_exploratory}\},

\Delta_{\mathrm{sup}}=\mathbb{E}[S\mid\pi=\texttt{imputed\_supported}]-\mathbb{E}[S\mid\pi=\texttt{observed}],

\Delta_{\mathrm{exp}}=\mathbb{E}[S\mid\pi=\texttt{imputed\_exploratory}]-\mathbb{E}[S\mid\pi=\texttt{observed}].

Monotonicity expectation, pre-specified: |\Delta_{\mathrm{exp}}|>|\Delta_{\mathrm{sup}}|. Violation is a result against the provenance account and is reported as such, not averaged away. Exploratory tracks carry non-informative weight for confirmatory claims.

B.10 Utility metrics

\mathrm{ER}@k=\frac{U_k(\mathcal{E})}{U_k(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{raw}})},\qquad
\Delta@k=U_k(\mathcal{E})-U_k(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{raw}}).

Zero-denominator rule (pre-specified): if U_k(\mathcal{S}_{\mathrm{raw}})=0, \mathrm{ER}@k is undefined and only \Delta@k is reported; no smoothing constant is introduced after seeing the data. Signed-cancellation rule: because \mathrm{ER}@k can cancel under signed effects, \Delta@k is always co-reported and is the primary quantity for the H4 decision.

B.11 Evidence score

E(v)=w_1\mathrm{Stab}(v)+w_2\mathrm{Conc}(v)+w_3\mathrm{Prov}(v)-w_4\mathrm{Dist}(v).

Admission requirement:

\Delta@k\big(E(v)\big)-\Delta@k\big(\text{raw}+\text{distance}\big)>\delta^{\mathrm{eq}}_{H4}\quad\text{on development data.}

Complexity audit reported: number of free parameters p_E, development-set size n_{\mathrm{dev}}, ratio p_E/n_{\mathrm{dev}}.

