Appendix F — Statistical Appendix

F.1 Cluster structure

Cluster definition (by gene, enhancer, locus, or guide) is fixed in advance. Reported for every analysis:

· raw cluster count G;
· full cluster-size distribution, not only the count;
· effective number of clusters;
· locus-level re-clustering sensitivity analysis.

If the H1 conclusion reverses under locus-level re-clustering, the conclusion is reported as fragile.

F.2 Wild cluster bootstrap

Test statistic with cluster-robust variance:

t=\frac{\hat{\beta}-\beta_0}{\sqrt{\hat{V}_{\mathrm{CV1}}}}.

Restricted residuals are imposed under the null; clusters are reweighted by six-point weights

w_g\in\left\{-\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{2}},\,-1,\,-\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}},\,\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}},\,1,\,\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{2}}\right\},

each with probability 1/6. The bootstrap distribution of t^{*} supplies the p-value and the interval. Six-point weights are the primary procedure for small G; a score-transformed variant is reported as a pre-specified sensitivity check. Balanced-cluster asymptotics are not relied upon.

F.3 Minimum detectable effect under clustering

\mathrm{MDE}=\left(z_{1-\alpha/2}+z_{1-\beta}\right)\cdot\sigma\sqrt{\frac{\mathrm{DE}}{Gm}},
\qquad \mathrm{DE}=1+(m-1)\rho .

Computed at \alpha=0.05 (two-sided), power 0.80, using the observed cluster-size distribution and an empirically anchored \rho. Balanced-cluster computations are reported only as an optimistic bound, never as the operative MDE.

F.4 Two one-sided tests

With margin bounds \delta_L,\delta_U:

t_L=\frac{\hat{\beta}-\delta_L}{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}},\qquad
t_U=\frac{\hat{\beta}-\delta_U}{\widehat{\mathrm{SE}}}.

Equivalence is concluded only if both one-sided nulls are rejected at level \alpha. Absence of significance is never treated as equivalence.

F.5 Multiplicity

Primary hypotheses each carry their own decision rule and are not adjusted. Secondary subgroup analyses are controlled by the Benjamini–Hochberg procedure at a pre-specified false-discovery rate.

F.6 Missing data

Complete-case logging only. Experimental outcomes are never imputed. Missingness is reported by dataset and by cluster.

