Raw output Stored unmodified, not only derived scores

H.2 Canary set

Fifty to one hundred fixed pairs, queried at the start and at the end of the evaluation period. Drift beyond a pre-specified tolerance triggers a pause and a reported drift note. Without this control, a null utility result cannot be distinguished from a silent model update.

H.3 Leakage controls

· L1 stochastic-pass seed fixed before data access;
· L2 primary-modality map fixed before data access;
· L3 k_{\mathrm{dev}}\neq k_{\mathrm{eval}}, both fixed;
· L4 normalisation constants frozen in the configuration file.

Leakage is silent. A seed, a modality map, a value of k, or a normalisation constant chosen after seeing evaluation data renders the metric partially in-sample, and no post-hoc correction recovers it.

H.4 Perturbation-operator discipline

Primary operator preserves single-base and dinucleotide composition; a substitution operator is rejected on distributional grounds. At least one alternative operator is reported as a sensitivity analysis, and non-survival under that analysis is reported.

H.5 Fixed pair set

All generation comparisons are scored on a fixed pair set. A comparison permitting different scored pairs is confounded by context length, and the apparent advantage of a larger-context model may be an artefact of seeing more sequence.

H.6 Primitive audit

Executed as a minimal public notebook, before main analysis, testing both:

1. whether the observed-track filter returns a strict subset, a null filter, or induces collapse;
2. whether per-track metadata remain consistent on the returned object.

Audit code is exempt from the no-code-before-preregistration rule because it is itself part of the frozen protocol.

