Appendix J — Epistemic Principles

J.1 Measurement

P-1. A metric measures what it measures, not what we want it to measure. Conflating rank, magnitude, and discrimination is a category error, not a simplification.

P-2. A slope alone does not classify a dataset. Under y^{\exp}=\alpha+\beta\hat{y} the sign of the error depends on \hat{y}; the crossing point, the fractions on each side, and region-wise error are reported.

P-3. No non-significant result is proof of no effect. Equivalence is tested, never inferred from absence of significance.

J.2 Design

P-4. Perturbation is not variation. Treating the difference between a CRISPRi ablation and a DNA variant as calibration error is a category error dressed as a metric.

P-5. A transformation must be pre-specified, finite, and falsifiable. Otherwise the transformation is a post-hoc fit to the evaluation set.

P-6. Dataset selection is a scientific eligibility decision. A public dataset is not automatically licensed, independent, or out-of-distribution.

J.3 Inference

P-7. Small-cluster inference requires small-cluster methods. Asymptotic cluster-robust standard errors are unreliable when clusters are few and imbalanced.

P-8. A difference in significance is not a significant difference. Generation contrasts are estimated directly, not inferred from two p-values.

P-9. Leakage is silent. Any quantity chosen after seeing evaluation data renders the metric partially in-sample, and no correction recovers it.

J.4 Honesty

P-10. The framework must be able to demonstrate its own irrelevance. A framework that can only confirm its utility is a persuasion device, not a scientific instrument.

P-11. Negative, equivalent, and inconclusive results are all reportable. The three-part decision structure exists so that no result is forced into a false binary.

P-12. Preregistration is the cheapest defence against post-hoc drift. It converts a framework we built into a hypothesis we tested.

P-13. Causal language is scoped to the intervention actually performed. CMAE is a claim about model input, not about biology.

J.5 Programme discipline

P-14. Measure the evidence base first; build the instrument second.

P-15. Verification against primary sources is not optional. Every factual correction in Appendix I was caught by verification; without it the programme would rest on a reversed slope, a mislabelled dataset, and a non-existent API field.

P-16. Every correction is logged, not overwritten. The intellectual trajectory is part of the scientific record.

P-17. Ambition is bounded by the evidence base before the evidence base is asked to support it.

